Fractol: A Fractal Visualization Project\par \pard\plain 
{\tc\tcl1 \v Fractol: A Fractal Visualization Project}
{\xe \v Fractol: A Fractal Visualization Project}
{\bkmkstart AAAAAAAALL}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAAALL}
{
\pard\plain \s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid {\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
{\bkmkstart AAAAAAAALM}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAAALM}
 Fractol is a fractal visualization tool written in C that leverages the MLX42 graphics library for rendering complex and stunning fractals. The project supports the generation of the Mandelbrot and Julia sets, with interactive controls for zooming, panning, and exploring these fascinating mathematical structures.\par
{\bkmkstart AAAAAAAALN}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAAALN}
{{\pard\plain \s2\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\kerning28\cgrid 
Overview
\par}
{\tc\tcl2 \v Overview}
Fractals are infinitely complex patterns that are self-similar across different scales. They are created by repeating a simple mathematical process over and over in an ongoing feedback loop. Fractol uses this principle to generate visually captivating fractal images.\par
{\bkmkstart AAAAAAAALO}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAAALO}
{{\pard\plain \s3\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\cgrid 
Supported Fractals
\par}
{\tc\tcl3 \v Supported Fractals}
{
\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
Mandelbrot Set: A set of complex numbers that do not diverge when iteratively applied to the equation {\f2 z_(n+1) = z_n^2 + c} , starting with {\f2 z_0 = 0} .\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
Julia Set: A family of fractals defined for a fixed complex constant {\f2 c} , iterating over {\f2 z_(n+1) = z_n^2 + c} .\par}
\par
\par}
\par}
{\bkmkstart AAAAAAAALP}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAAALP}
{{\pard\plain \s2\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\kerning28\cgrid 
Mathematical Foundation
\par}
{\tc\tcl2 \v Mathematical Foundation}
{\bkmkstart AAAAAAAALQ}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAAALQ}
{{\pard\plain \s3\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\cgrid 
Complex Numbers
\par}
{\tc\tcl3 \v Complex Numbers}
Fractals rely on complex numbers, which have two components:{
\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{\b Real part (Re)} \par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{\b Imaginary part (Im)} \par}
\par
Complex numbers can be represented as {\f2 z = a + bi} , where:{
\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{\f2 a}  is the real component.\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{\f2 b}  is the imaginary component.\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
{\f2 i}  is the square root of -1.\par}
\par
\par}
{\bkmkstart AAAAAAAALR}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAAALR}
{{\pard\plain \s3\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\cgrid 
Iteration and Divergence
\par}
{\tc\tcl3 \v Iteration and Divergence}
The fundamental concept in fractal generation is iteration:{
\par
\pard\plain \s140\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\fs20\cgrid 
1.\tab Start with an initial complex number {\f2 z_0} .\par
\pard\plain \s140\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\fs20\cgrid 
2.\tab Iteratively apply the equation {\f2 z_(n+1) = z_n^2 + c} .\par
\pard\plain \s140\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\fs20\cgrid 
3.\tab Stop if the magnitude of {\f2 z} , given by {\f2 |z|^2} , is greater than 4 (escape condition) or if the maximum number of iterations is reached.\par}
\par
The number of iterations before escape determines the color of a pixel, creating the fractal's intricate patterns.\par}
{\bkmkstart AAAAAAAALS}
{\bkmkend AAAAAAAALS}
{{\pard\plain \s3\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\cgrid 
Coordinate Mapping
\par}
{\tc\tcl3 \v Coordinate Mapping}
The fractal exists in the complex plane, but pixels are rendered in a 2D screen coordinate system. Fractol maps each pixel to a corresponding complex number based on:{
\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
The screen resolution.\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
The zoom level.\par
\pard\plain \s120\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec }\ls1\adjustright \fs20\cgrid 
The viewport offset.\par}
\par
\par}
\par}
